  "worked_solution": "The polar coordinates of $Q$ are $(r,\\theta )=(5,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $Q$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.5 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$",
